      "knowledge_point_id": "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068172/assets/4e343d2884532c006e83cd4c90e70a6cd9af9247194c8a7f8832ef8cbaf293b5.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=2.$",
