      "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{x-2}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{5}{2})=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$",
