    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-211282",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve",
      "problem": "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2$ and the point $P(5,2)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(5,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(4)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-32+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
